<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keōpūolani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1778–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Keopuolani, Kalanikauikaʻalaneo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keōpūolani was the highest-ranking wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, a chiefess of naha rank, born of a royal half-brother and half-sister marriage, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> required commoners to fall prostrate before her. Daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kīwalaʻō</a>, king of Hawaiʻi island, she was caught with her grandmother Kalola after Kamehameha's victory at Kepaniwai on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1790, and married him in 1795, linking his house to the ruling houses of Maui and Hawaiʻi. She bore Kamehameha II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> and Princess Nāhiʻenaʻena. In 1819 she joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a> and Hewahewa in sharing a meal of forbidden foods to break the kapu, and she was baptised a Christian just before her death in 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keopuolani.jpg" alt="Keōpūolani" width="330" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The American Tract Society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Christian_Queen,_page_2_(1855)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
